Yes, flood damage from a creek or river overflow is covered, but subject to specific exclusions.

**Coverage:** "Flood" is defined as "the covering of normally dry land by water that has escaped or been released from the normal confines of: any lake, or any river, creek or other natural watercourse, whether or not altered or modified; or any reservoir, canal or dam" [RAA Contents PDS, page 87]. The policy covers "loss or damage to the Insured Property provide as a result of Flood" [RAA Contents PDS, page 50].

**Exclusions:** Flood cover does not apply to loss or damage:
- to any part of the Home that is not fully built;
- to tennis courts;
- to a wharf, jetty, pontoon or sea wall;
- to water in a tank, swimming pool or spa, including cleaning costs;
- to swimming pools or spas and their covers or liners (except permanently attached hard lid style pool covers);
- to paint where there is no structural damage caused by Flood to other parts of the Home;
- caused by underground (hydrostatic) water;
- to a glasshouse or greenhouse;
- to unsealed paths or driveways (e.g., gravel or loose materials);
- caused by Flood arising from deliberate or Malicious Damage to, or destruction of, a reservoir or dam; and/or
- to a path or driveway (and other items, per an incomplete list) if not structurally sound or not reasonably maintained prior to the loss
[RAA Contents PDS, page 50].

Additionally, Flood excludes water from Actions of the Sea, except where caused by a Storm Surge occurring at the same time the Insured Property is damaged by a Storm, or by a Tsunami. Flood also excludes a mixture of water from Actions of the Sea and water from any other source [RAA Contents PDS, page 88].

Note: The excerpt listing exclusions on page 50 appears cut off, so there may be additional exclusions not shown in the provided material.
